Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 23:02:34 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 113/126] data: an object's colour reads as WE's vec3 field, "1
 1 1" is white
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

WE parses an image, text or light layer's `color` (and `clearcolor`, `ambientcolor`) with one field
parser, GH 0x1401d0bc0: atof at the start of the string and after each run of spaces, three times,
missing components 0; a JSON number sets all three. Whole numbers are not special, so "1 1 1" is
white. LWE read a colour without a decimal point as 0..255 bytes, so "1 1 1" came out 1/255 —
black where WE draws white (the library writes "1.00000 …", a hand-written scene doesn't).

ColorBuilder::parseObject is WE's rule; JsonExtensions::color uses it. The old reading stays for a
particle `colorrandom`'s min / max (JsonExtensions::colorBytes), whose values WE treats as bytes
(the instanceoverride `color` is divided by 255 into `colorn`, GH 0x140251xxx), and for a colour user
property above 1 (ColorBuilder::parseProperty, as before). A "#rrggbb" string keeps LWE's hex reading.

Origin: ours
Why: docs/TODO.md "a layer colour written without a decimal point ("1 1 1") is read as 0–255 bytes"
Status: candidate-for-upstream
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
